//RS // ── D-CANCELMODEL1=C: preemptive cancellation at wait points ─────────────────
//RS // One unwind mechanism, two triggers (deadline E3003, task cancel). A cancelled
//RS // task unwinds at its next wait point by panicking with the `JetCancelUnwind`
//RS // marker; the task runner maps that payload to a `Cancelled` result and every
//RS // Drop-backed cleanup runs on the way out — the same shape a blown deadline
//RS // already produces. A shielded region (SHIELD_DEPTH > 0) DEFERS the unwind: wait
//RS // points inside it complete normally and the deferred cancel/deadline lands when
//RS // the outermost region exits. D-SHIELDNAME1=A (ratified 2026-07-11) spells this
//RS // region `#Shield { … }`; codegen lowers the block to
//RS // `jet_scheduler_shield_enter`/`_leave` around the body (Codegen/TIR emit).
//RS //
//RS // Cleanup rule (#2007): a thread that is already unwinding is past the point
//RS // where an interrupt can be delivered. Its Drop-backed cleanup is running and
//RS // the frame it unwinds to is the one that reports the outcome, so a raise from
//RS // drop glue is a double panic — which Rust turns into `panic in a destructor
//RS // during cleanup` and a process abort, not an outcome. An in-flight unwind is
//RS // therefore a critical section in exactly the D-CANCELMODEL1=C sense, and
//RS // `jet_scheduler_shielded` reports it as one: wait points reached from cleanup
//RS // complete normally, the interrupt stays recorded (a cancel on
//RS // `JetTaskControl.cancelled`, a blown deadline on
//RS // `jet_task_deadline_mark_pending`), and it lands on the in-flight unwind's own
//RS // outcome instead of starting a second one.
//RS struct JetCancelUnwind;
//RS 
//RS struct JetDeadlineUnwind {
//RS     rendered: String,
//RS }
//RS 
