//RS /// Catch an unwind inside a frame that foreign code sits *below*.
//RS ///
//RS /// D-JITUNWIND1 (#1997): `cranelift-jit` registers no unwind information for
//RS /// the code it emits, so a JIT frame carries no FDE and a Rust panic raised
//RS /// above one can never be walked past — libgcc's phase 1 returns
//RS /// `_URC_END_OF_STACK` and std rtaborts with `failed to initiate panic,
//RS /// error 5` before any outer `catch_unwind` exists to see it. The answer is
//RS /// conversion at the boundary: catch inside the host frame, hand back a
//RS /// status, and let native code leave through its own control flow. This is the
//RS /// catch half; [`jet_scheduler_classify_unwind`] is the conversion half.
//RS ///
//RS /// The catching flag is set for one reason only: the process panic hook must
//RS /// stay quiet for a control transfer this frame converts
//RS /// (`jet_scheduler_suppress_panic_report`). The wait-point bookkeeping that
//RS /// [`jet_scheduler_wait_without_unwind`] adds is deliberately absent — an
//RS /// ordinary host seam is not a wait point and must not make one look live.
